__pycache__/
*.pyc
node_modules/
.claude-flow/

# Stray 0-byte files — see CLAUDE.md.
#
# A fragment of JavaScript can reach cmd.exe, which reads the ">" in an "=>"
# arrow as an output redirection and drops an empty file named after the next
# token. These are harmless on their own, but they accumulate as untracked noise
# and a broad `git add` will eventually commit one. Everything below is anchored
# with a leading "/" so it only applies to the repo root, where they land.
#
# Avoiding inline `node -e` reduces these but does not stop them: they also
# appear from script files, from text echoed to the terminal, and from messages
# between sessions. This is the guard that actually holds.

# 1. Names carrying JavaScript punctuation. No legitimate file here contains
#    these characters, so the patterns are safe to keep broad. A literal "["
#    has to be written "[[]" because bare brackets open a character class.
/*(*
/*)*
/*[[]*
/*]*
/*{*
/*}*
/*$*
/*'*
/*`*
/*%*
/*=*

# 2. Bare identifiers and numbers. These look like ordinary filenames, so they
#    are listed individually rather than pattern-matched — a wildcard here could
#    hide something real. Add to this list if a new one shows up.
/a
/s
/cs2
/48px
/150
/6817
/9054
/SciVal
/x.type
/c.emailStatus
